<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madrasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A madrasa is an Islamic institution of higher learning, typically teaching law, theology and related sciences. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, notably under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, founded influential madrasas to train scholars and officials and promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> continued this policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrasa.jpg" alt="Madrasa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain; derivative work by MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Madrasa is the Arabic word for any educational institution, secular or religious, and means a place where learning and studying take place. In Arabic it can refer to a primary or secondary school as well as a university-level school. In English it usually refers to Islamic institutions of learning, and historians use it for a college where Islamic law was taught along with other subjects. In medieval usage the term was usually specific to higher learning, as opposed to elementary schools, which were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">kuttab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalwa">khalwa</a> or maktab.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that the first madrasa education took place under Muhammad at the estate of Zayd ibn Arqam, and that after the Hijrah a school known as Suffa was set up beside the mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. The formal madrasa developed in the late 11th century under the Abbasid caliphs, when the Seljuk vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> created a system of state madrasas, called the Nizamiyahs after him, in cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. Nizam al-Mulk was later murdered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>.</p><p>Madrasa-type institutions appear to have existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> before him, but his period is considered by many to be the starting point for the spread of the formal madrasa across the Muslim world, adapted to all four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> legal schools and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders. Part of the motive of Sunni rulers and elites in adopting it was a wish to counter the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> by spreading Sunni teachings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In its formative period the madrasa curriculum included only the religious sciences, and philosophy and the secular sciences were often excluded. The curriculum slowly diversified, and many later madrasas taught logic, mathematics and philosophy, and some history, ethics, medicine, astronomy and chemistry. The founder usually set the curriculum. Between 1155 and 1260 there were 75 madrasas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, 51 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and up to 44 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><p>Architecturally, the classical madrasa was centred on an interior courtyard with four iwans, vaulted chambers open on one side, arranged symmetrically around it. Practically none of the first Nizamiyah madrasas survives. Many Seljuk madrasas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> survive, often with an attached mausoleum, a minaret and an ornate entrance portal, and some courtyards were covered by a dome, as at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karatay-madrasa">Karatay Madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, founded in 1279.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> madrasas flourished only in the 13th century under the Marinid and Hafsid dynasties, which followed the Almohad dynasty. The earliest Hafsid madrasa was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1252, and the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was built in Fes in 1271. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> madrasas trained doctors, engineers, lawyers and religious officials, among other members of the governing elite, for nearly six centuries. Muhammad the Conqueror built eight higher madrasas for specialised studies and eight lower ones beside a mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madrasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ayyubid sultans patronised religious scholarship, endowed <b>madrasas</b> that consolidated Sunni learning in former Fatimid Egypt, and restored the Dome of the Rock and the al-Aqsa Mosque to Muslim worship after the reconquest of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jama Masjid in the fort of Gulbarga, built in 1367, is unusual for having its entire courtyard covered by domes, while the <b>madrasa</b> built by Mahmud Gawan at Bidar in 1472, with its tile-faced minarets, and the painted royal tombs at Ashtur reflect strong Iranian influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Contemporary historians describe great quantities of gold, jewels, captives and elephants brought back to Ghazni after each campaign, and some of this wealth was spent on a great mosque, a <b>madrasa</b> and palaces in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Zakariya founded the Shamma’iyya, generally regarded as the first <b>madrasa</b> in Ifriqiya, and his successors endowed further colleges, libraries and the Zaytuna Mosque, whose teaching circles drew students from across the Maghreb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kara-Khanids generally respected the Hanafi scholars of Bukhara and Samarqand, built mosques, caravanserais and <b>madrasas</b>, and endowed religious foundations, although relations between khans and the ulama were sometimes tense.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A large <b>madrasa</b> and library stood at the cathedral mosque in Kazan, and Kazan became a centre of science and theology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
